---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StackMap from "../components/StackMap.astro";

import ToolsDiagram from "../components/diagrams/ToolsDiagram.astro";
import { composition, developerExperience, packages, templateOwn, templatePlain, templateStart } from "../data/building-blocks";
const anchor = (name: string) => name.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, "-");
---
<Base
  signoff
  title="Building blocks: stop rediscovering what you need to build · inixiative"
  description="Start a product with the hard parts already built, and use the pieces for the most common problems on their own: who can do what, rules, and the steps a record moves through."
  domain="building-blocks"
  current="building-blocks"
>
  <section class="hero anchor-target" id="primitives">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Building blocks</span>
    <h1 class="display-1">Stop rediscovering what you need to build.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The same shapes of problem come up in product after product. Usually each one gets a one-off
      solution, often right next to another problem with the same shape. Each building block takes one
      of those problems and solves it once, in a shape you can reuse wherever it shows up.
    </p>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        Template is where they come together. For the problems almost every product has, such as
        deciding who can do what, the pieces also work without the template: a rules engine, a
        permissions library, the screens for building rules, and a way to say which step a record can
        move to next. Most of them already run in production.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="byline">Aron Greenspan</p>
    <p class="meta">If your coding agent writes its own versions instead, <a class="text-link" href="/agentic/">Foundry</a> points it back at these.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section anchor-target" id="template">
    <div class="card" data-raised>
      <div class="card-head">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>template</span>
        <span class="tag" data-tone="open">MIT</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="display-3">A SaaS app where the hard parts are already built.</h2>
      <p>Template is like shadcn, but for the whole SaaS app: you start a product from it and own the code. On day one:</p>
      <ul>
        {templatePlain.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <div class="card-links">
        <a class="text-link" href="/building-blocks/technical/#template">Technical details</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/inixiative/template">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section anchor-target" id="starting-from-template">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Starting from template</span>
      <h2>Start a product from template, and own all of it.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Template is a working SaaS app with the parts nearly every product needs already built. You copy it
        once and build your product in that copy. Nothing stays tied to a service of ours. Kingdom, the hosted
        hub on the <a class="text-link" href="/agentic/">agentic</a> side, started from template the same way.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="text">
        <h3>How you start</h3>
        <ol class="steps">
          {templateStart.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.step}</strong>{s.command && <code>{s.command}</code>} {s.detail}</li>)}
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <h3>What you own</h3>
        <ul>
          {templateOwn.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p>
          Template has no plugin system and no hidden framework. Each solved problem is ordinary code in your
          repository, documented where it lives, so you can read it, change it or replace it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>The pieces</span>
      <h2>For the problems almost every product has.</h2>
      <p class="lede">You can use each one without the template.</p>
    </div>
    <StackMap view="whole" focus="building-blocks" />
    <ul class="packages">
      {packages.map((p) => (
        <li class="package" id={anchor(p.name)}>
          <div class="package-name">
            <h3>{p.name}</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="package-body">
            <p class="line">{p.plain}</p>
            <div class="card-links">
              <a class="text-link" href={`/building-blocks/technical/#${anchor(p.name)}`}>Technical details</a>
              {p.demo && <a class="text-link" href={p.demo}>Live demo</a>}
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section anchor-target" id="how-they-fit">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>How they fit together</span>
      <h2>One rule language, from the database to the screen.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Each piece reads the one beneath it. A rule a tenant writes in the builder is the same JSON that
        permissions checks and that the database query filters by, so the three never disagree.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="pitches">
      {composition.map((c) => (
        <li class="pitch">
          <h3>{c.name}</h3>
          <p>{c.does}</p>
          {c.href && <div class="card-links"><a class="text-link" href={c.href}>Try the rule builder</a></div>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section anchor-target" id="developer-experience">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Developer experience</span>
      <h2>Tools for working on the code.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        These tools help while you build, and none of them ends up in your product. Atlas keeps a map of what
        is where in the code. Gloss keeps notes about the code out of the code, and hands them to an
        agent when it reads that file. Config gives every project the same setup.
      </p>
      <p>
        Template comes with its own: several copies of a project can run side by side without colliding,
        a guided setup creates a new project's accounts and services, and automatic checks stop code that
        rebuilds something the project already has.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="area-figure"><ToolsDiagram /></figure>
    <ul class="packages">
      {developerExperience.map((p) => (
        <li class="package" id={anchor(p.name)}>
          <div class="package-name">
            <h3>{p.name}</h3>
          </div>
          <div class="package-body">
            <p class="line">{p.plain}</p>
            <div class="card-links">
              <a class="text-link" href={`/building-blocks/technical/#${anchor(p.name)}`}>Technical details</a>
              {p.demo && <a class="text-link" href={p.demo}>Live demo</a>}
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="meta"><a class="text-link" href="/building-blocks/technical/">Technical details for every piece</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" data-compact>
    <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Paused</span>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card anchor-target" id="conditional-form" data-plain>
        <div class="card-head"><h3>conditional-form</h3><span class="tag" data-tone="progress">Paused</span></div>
        <p>Forms whose show, hide and validation logic is the same rules the server uses. Not recommended for use.</p>
        <div class="card-links"><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/inixiative/conditional-form">GitHub</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
